consiSt("nd",{"courts":[{"l":"HIGHEST COURT","n":"North Dakota Supreme Court","d":"The state’s appellate court for nearly all purposes: civil appeals go straight from the district court to the Supreme Court by a notice filed with its clerk. It adopts the procedural rules (civil, appellate, Rules of Court, administrative rules), and the power to admit lawyers is vested in it by statute. Review of a decision is by petition for rehearing within 14 days (N.D.R.App.P. 3, 4, 40; N.D.C.C. §§ 27-11-02, 28-27-01)."},{"l":"INTERMEDIATE","n":"Temporary Court of Appeals","d":"Not a standing court. Three-judge panels (retired justices, active or retired district judges, lawyers, or surrogate judges) hear only cases the Supreme Court assigns, usually error-correction categories such as family-law decrees, misdemeanors, small claims, municipal and administrative appeals, and summary-judgment appeals. All filings still go to the clerk of the Supreme Court. Review is by discretionary petition to the Supreme Court within 14 days. The statute is effective through January 1, 2034 (N.D.C.C. ch. 27-02.1; N.D. Sup. Ct. Admin. R. 27)."},{"l":"TRIAL COURTS","n":"District Courts","d":"The single trial court of general jurisdiction, organized into eight judicial districts (Northwest, North Central, Northeast, Northeast Central, East Central, Southeast, South Central, Southwest), each with a presiding judge. County courts were abolished effective January 1995 and their work moved to the district courts. District judges serve six-year terms. Civil practice follows the N.D.R.Civ.P., the N.D. Rules of Court, and district local rules (N.D.C.C. §§ 27-05-00.1, 27-05-02, 27-05-06; N.D. Sup. Ct. Admin. R. 6; N.D.R.Civ.P. 83)."},{"l":"LIMITED JURISDICTION","n":"Small Claims Court; Municipal Courts","d":"Small claims court is the district court sitting under N.D.C.C. ch. 27-08.1 for money claims and certain contract-cancellation claims up to $15,000. Assignees may not file. A plaintiff who chooses small claims cannot appeal, and the defendant gives up appeal unless it elects to remove the case to district court. Municipal courts hear city matters; N.D.R.Civ.P. 60 applies to them for relief from judgments (N.D.C.C. §§ 27-08.1-01, 27-08.1-02, 27-08.1-04; N.D.R.Civ.P. 60(f))."}],"slapp":[{"t":"No anti-SLAPP statute found in the Century Code","d":"A search of the Legislative Branch’s full Century Code section index (all titles, chapter and section headings) found no anti-SLAPP law, no Uniform Public Expression Protection Act, and no section on strategic lawsuits, public participation in litigation, or public expression. North Dakota therefore has no special motion to dismiss, discovery stay, or interlocutory appeal for speech-based suits. The check was of headings, not every section’s full text.","c":"N.D.C.C. index (ndlegis.gov), checked November 10, 2026","v":true},{"t":"Closest tools: mandatory fees for frivolous claims, and Rule 11","d":"In civil actions the court must award reasonable actual and statutory costs, including attorney’s fees, to the prevailing party on finding a claim for relief frivolous: so completely lacking in facts or law that a reasonable person could not have expected to win. The prevailing party must have alleged the frivolous nature of the claim in its responsive pleading, so a defendant must plead it in the answer. A good-faith argument to extend, modify, or reverse the law is protected. A party pleading allegations or denials without reasonable cause and not in good faith, found untrue, pays the other side’s resulting expenses and fees. Rule 11 sanctions (with a 21-day safe harbor) and Rule 12(b)(6) dismissal are otherwise the ordinary routes.","c":"N.D.C.C. §§ 28-26-01(2), 28-26-31; N.D.R.Civ.P. 11(c), 12(b)(6)","v":true},{"t":"Repeat litigants: statewide vexatious-litigant pre-filing orders","d":"A presiding judge, on request or on the court’s own motion, may designate a vexatious litigant (for example, two adverse litigations involving vexatious conduct in seven years, repeated relitigation, or a prior designation by any court) and enter a pre-filing order requiring leave of court for new filings, possibly with security. The order must award the costs and reasonable attorney’s fees of responding, including the cost of obtaining the order. The litigant has 14 days to respond to the proposed order. A designation by a presiding judge is appealable.","c":"N.D. Sup. Ct. Admin. R. 58, §§ 2-6","v":true}],"oos":[{"t":"Pro hac vice: motion, affidavit, Board filing, and fee before you appear","d":"Every nonresident lawyer licensed elsewhere who appears in person, signs pleadings, or is designated as counsel in a North Dakota state court, agency, or tribunal needs pro hac vice admission. Before appearing, the lawyer must designate a North Dakota-licensed associate lawyer, file a motion and supporting affidavit with the trial court clerk (or hearing or presiding officer), file copies with the State Board of Law Examiners at the same time, and pay the Board the annual license fee charged to a North Dakota lawyer licensed five or more years (or certify it was already paid that calendar year). The rule does not state the dollar amount; the statute caps the annual license fee at $400, but the current figure was not collected.\nThe affidavit must list every jurisdiction of admission and years admitted, any pending discipline, restriction, or probation, any past suspension or disbarment, the number of North Dakota actions in the past three years, and contact information.\nDeadline: the motion must be filed no later than 45 days after service of the pleading, motion, or other paper.\nA lawyer currently suspended, disbarred, or under license restriction anywhere is ineligible.","c":"Admission to Practice R. 3(A)(1)-(2), (D); N.D.C.C. § 27-11-22","v":true},{"t":"Associate counsel must attend; five appearances a year is presumed excessive","d":"The North Dakota associate lawyer’s name and address must appear on all papers, and the associate must appear personally and stay in attendance with the nonresident lawyer at all appearances unless excused. Permission is needed separately in each action. More than five pro hac vice appearances in the preceding 12 months is presumed excessive (pro bono work excepted); exceeding it requires good cause tied to the client’s welfare, such as a specialized field, a long-standing relationship, or lack of qualified local counsel.\nOn appeal of the same action, file only an updated affidavit with the clerk of the Supreme Court (no new fee unless it is a new calendar year); otherwise a new motion, affidavit, and fee are needed. Representing a client in an in-state mediation or arbitration also requires filing the affidavit and fee with the Board first.","c":"Admission to Practice R. 3(A)(3)-(6)","v":true},{"t":"Temporary practice and in-house counsel: written disclosure to the client","d":"Rule 5.5 permits temporary in-state services on the usual ABA-style grounds, but carves out any work that requires pro hac vice admission or registration. A lawyer practicing under Rule 5.5(b) or (c) must disclose in writing to the client that the lawyer is not licensed in North Dakota, a requirement not in the ABA Model Rule. In-house counsel with a systematic and continuous presence must register annually with the Board (affidavit, employer affidavit, fee, 36 CLE hours per three years). Registration lasts at most five years or until the lawyer becomes eligible for admission on motion.","c":"N.D.R. Prof. Conduct 5.5(b)-(d); Admission to Practice R. 3(C)","v":true},{"t":"Limited-scope work: ghostwriting allowed; limited appearances need notice and a completion certificate","d":"A lawyer complying with Rule 1.2 may prepare filings for a self-represented party without appearing; the party signs as “self-represented.” A lawyer making a limited appearance must serve a notice stating its precise scope (amended if the scope grows). Within 14 days after finishing, the lawyer files a Certificate of Completion of Limited Appearance, which operates as withdrawal without court approval. While the limited representation is in effect, papers must be served on both the party and the lawyer. Other withdrawals require leave of court, with notice to the client by personal service, certified or registered mail, or traceable carrier; after an appeal is filed, the withdrawal motion goes to the Supreme Court clerk.","c":"N.D.R.Ct. 11.9, 11.2; N.D.R.Civ.P. 5(b)(2)(A)","v":true},{"t":"E-filing: Odyssey in district court, a separate system in the Supreme Court","d":"All documents in civil cases must be e-filed through Odyssey (self-represented litigants and prisoners exempt). Every attorney must give the State Board of Law Examiners an e-mail address for service, which is posted on the Supreme Court website; service to it is complete on transmission, and an attorney who fails to provide or open e-mail is treated as served. A document submitted by 11:59 p.m. local time is filed that day; if it is rejected, time is tolled and a resubmission within 3 days is timely. Filing fees are paid by card at filing. All paragraphs in filed documents must be numbered. The Supreme Court uses its own electronic filing system (11:59 p.m. Bismarck time). How a pro hac vice lawyer gets e-filing credentials is not addressed in the collected texts.","c":"N.D.R.Ct. 3.5(a)-(e); N.D.R.Civ.P. 5(b)(1), (d)(1); N.D.R.App.P. 25","v":true}],"ethics":[{"t":"Governing code: North Dakota Rules of Professional Conduct","d":"North Dakota’s rules follow ABA Model Rule numbering (cited N.D.R. Prof. Conduct), with several substantive departures shown below and a state-specific Rule 1.19 on client files and Rule 4.5 on inadvertently sent documents.","c":"N.D.R. Prof. Conduct 1.0-9.1","v":true},{"t":"Confidentiality: disclosure to prevent death or serious harm is mandatory","d":"A lawyer is required (not merely permitted, as under ABA Model Rule 1.6(b)(1)) to reveal information to the extent reasonably necessary to prevent reasonably certain death or substantial bodily harm. The permissive exceptions for client crime or fraud using the lawyer’s services, ethics advice, self-defense, law or court order, and conflict checks track the ABA list.","c":"N.D.R. Prof. Conduct 1.6(b)-(d)","v":true},{"t":"Lateral screening is conditional","d":"A firm can screen a lateral lawyer with a former-client conflict only if the confidential information is unlikely to be significant, there is no reasonably apparent risk its use will materially harm the former client, the lawyer is timely screened and gets no part of the fee, and written notice goes to affected clients. The ABA rule has no “unlikely to be significant” or “material adverse effect” conditions. Imputed disqualification can otherwise be waived by client consent after consultation, except for a claim by one firm client against another in the same litigation.","c":"N.D.R. Prof. Conduct 1.10(b), (d)","v":true},{"t":"Fees: nonrefundable fees banned; flat fees may go to operating; contingent-fee closing statement","d":"Nonrefundable fees and nonrefundable retainers are prohibited, as is any agreement restricting the client’s right to fire the lawyer or unreasonably restricting refunds of unearned fees. A flat, fixed, or minimum fee for future services becomes the lawyer’s property when paid and may be deposited in the operating account; other advance fees go to trust. Contingent-fee agreements must be signed by the client and identify expenses owed win or lose, and a written closing statement is required. Fees may not be split with, or referral fees paid to, legal assistants.","c":"N.D.R. Prof. Conduct 1.5(c), (f)-(i), 1.15(c)","v":true},{"t":"No retaining lien on client files","d":"A lawyer may not assert a retaining lien on client files, papers, or property, or condition their return on payment of fees or copying costs. The rule defines what belongs to the client (including filed pleadings, correspondence, and items of evidentiary value even if unpaid) and what is lawyer work product. Copying charges after termination are allowed only if the client agreed in writing beforehand.","c":"N.D.R. Prof. Conduct 1.19","v":true},{"t":"Trust accounts: IOLTA, approved banks with overdraft reporting, and malpractice-insurance disclosure","d":"Nominal or short-term client funds go into a pooled interest-bearing trust account whose interest is paid to the North Dakota Bar Foundation. Trust accounts may be kept only at Disciplinary Board-approved institutions that agree to report overdrafts. Records must be kept six years. With annual license renewal, each lawyer certifies compliance and states whether the lawyer represents private clients and carries professional liability insurance (insurer and policy number), must report a lapse within 30 days, and the information is public on request. Out-of-state lawyers with qualifying out-of-state IOLTA-type accounts are exempt from the account-location requirement.","c":"N.D.R. Prof. Conduct 1.15(f)-(l)","v":true},{"t":"Misconduct: bias rule in 8.4(f), no ABA 8.4(g)","d":"North Dakota has not adopted ABA Model Rule 8.4(g). Instead, conduct prejudicial to the administration of justice includes knowingly manifesting bias or prejudice based on race, sex, religion, national origin, disability, age, or sexual orientation while representing a client, unless it is legitimate advocacy. Rule 8.4(g) makes conduct listed in the Century Code as a ground for revocation or suspension also misconduct. An inadvertently sent document triggers only a duty to promptly notify the sender.","c":"N.D.R. Prof. Conduct 8.4(f)-(g), 4.5","v":true}],"style":"State of North Dakota","home":"https://www.ndcourts.gov/","unique":{"mines":[{"t":"The case starts on service, and the defendant can force you to file in 20 days","d":"A civil action is commenced by service of the summons, not by filing. A defendant may serve a demand that the plaintiff file; if the complaint is not filed within 20 days after service of the demand, the service of the summons is void (relief only by motion within 60 days on excusable neglect). When you do file, proof of Rule 4 service must accompany the initiating pleading.","c":"N.D.R.Civ.P. 3, 5(d)(2)(A)"},{"t":"Appeal time runs 60 days from service of notice of entry, and the notice goes to the Supreme Court clerk","d":"File the notice of appeal with the clerk of the Supreme Court within 60 days from service of notice of entry of judgment, together with the transcript order. The prevailing party must serve notice of entry within 14 days. Do not wait for service if you have acted on the judgment: the record showing your own actual knowledge of entry can start the clock. An extension (up to 30 days) must be sought within 30 days after the deadline.","c":"N.D.R.App.P. 3(a), 4(a)(1), (a)(4), 10(b)(1)(E); N.D.R.Civ.P. 58(b)"},{"t":"Ten days to demand a new judge, and one ruling ends the right","d":"The no-cause demand for change of judge must be filed within 10 days after notice of the judge’s assignment for trial, notice of a trial date, or service of that judge’s ex parte order, whichever comes first. It is barred once the judge rules on any matter in which your client was heard or could have been heard, so even a routine motion hearing can forfeit it. The client must sign, or the attorney signs with permission and files a certificate of mailing a copy to the client.","c":"N.D.C.C. § 29-15-21(1)-(4)"},{"t":"Motion practice runs on briefs: 14 days to answer, and silence is an admission","d":"The movant serves and files a notice and brief; the opponent has 14 days after service to serve and file an answer brief, and the reply is due 7 days later. The motion is then decided on the briefs unless a party requests a hearing within 7 days after the answer-brief deadline, and the requester must secure a hearing time within 14 days or the request is waived. Failing to file an answer brief may be treated as an admission that the motion has merit. Extensions require a written order.","c":"N.D.R.Ct. 3.2(a), (c), (d)"},{"t":"Summary judgment has its own outer deadlines and page limits","d":"The motion and supporting papers must be filed at least 90 days before trial and 45 days before the hearing unless ordered otherwise. The response is due 30 days after service of the brief, and the reply 14 days later. Principal and answer briefs are limited to 38 pages, replies to 12 (footnotes included); a request to exceed must be filed at least 7 days before the brief is due.","c":"N.D.R.Civ.P. 56(c)(1)-(2)"},{"t":"Jury demand within 14 days, and you get six jurors unless you ask for nine","d":"Serve a written jury demand no later than 14 days after the last pleading directed to the issue, and file it; otherwise the jury is waived. A civil jury has six members unless a party specifically demands nine within the same 14 days. Each side gets only four peremptory challenges regardless of the number of parties.","c":"N.D.R.Civ.P. 38(b)-(c), (e), 47(c)(1), 48(b)"},{"t":"Medical negligence: expert affidavit within three months of commencement","d":"An action alleging professional negligence by a physician, nurse, hospital, licensed care facility, or other health care organization must be dismissed without prejudice on motion unless the plaintiff serves an affidavit with an admissible expert opinion supporting a prima facie case within three months after commencement. An extension requires good cause and a request made before the three months run. Obvious-occurrence cases (wrong patient or body part, retained foreign object) are excepted.","c":"N.D.C.C. § 28-01-46"},{"t":"Plead frivolousness in the answer if you want mandatory fees","d":"The court must award the prevailing party costs and attorney’s fees for a frivolous claim, but only if the prevailing party alleged the claim’s frivolous nature in its responsive pleading. Leaving it out of the answer forfeits the mandatory award.","c":"N.D.C.C. § 28-26-01(2)"},{"t":"Pro hac vice: 45-day filing window and a five-case cap","d":"The motion and affidavit must be filed no later than 45 days after service of the pleading, motion, or paper, with copies and the fee sent to the State Board of Law Examiners at the same time. More than five pro hac vice appearances within 12 months is presumed excessive. The North Dakota associate must appear with you at every appearance unless excused.","c":"Admission to Practice R. 3(A)(1)-(4)"},{"t":"Number every paragraph and cite the record by index number","d":"Every electronically filed trial-court document prepared by the parties must number its paragraphs, and references must be to paragraph numbers, not pages. On appeal, record citations must use the register-of-actions index format, for example (R156:12), and “Id.” may not be used for record citations. Briefs that do not comply will not be filed.","c":"N.D.R.Ct. 3.5(b)(2); N.D.R.App.P. 30, 32(a)(7), (c)"},{"t":"Post-trial motions run from notice of entry, and some cannot be extended","d":"Renewed JMOL, amended findings, and motions to alter or amend are due 28 days after notice of entry of judgment, and the court cannot extend those times. A new-trial motion is due within 60 days (6 months for newly discovered evidence). Costs require a verified statement within 30 days after the order for judgment, and an attorney’s-fee motion is due 21 days after notice of entry.","c":"N.D.R.Civ.P. 6(b)(2), 50(b), 52(b), 54(e), 59(c), (j)"}],"judge":[{"t":"Peremptory demand for change of judge: 10 days, and lost once the judge rules","d":"Any party to a civil or criminal action in district court may remove the assigned judge without cause by filing a written demand with the clerk. The party signs personally, or the attorney signs with permission and certifies that a copy was mailed to the client. The demand must state it is in good faith and not for delay, describe the action, name the judge, and certify that the judge has not ruled on any matter in which the party was heard or had the chance to be heard.\nIt is invalid unless filed within 10 days after the earliest of: notice of assignment or reassignment of a judge for trial, notice that a trial has been scheduled, or service of an ex parte order signed by that judge. A later-added party has 10 days after the next triggering event, or after being added. No demand may be made after the judge has ruled on any matter in which the party was heard or could have been heard. Proceedings to modify support, property division, or custody count as separate proceedings.\nOn filing, the judge is immediately disqualified and may not rule on timeliness; only the presiding judge of the district can invalidate the demand. A demand against a replacement judge must be made within 5 days after notice of the assignment, and the presiding judge may refuse a second demand from a party whose interests are not adverse to the first demander.","c":"N.D.C.C. § 29-15-21","v":true},{"t":"Recusal for cause: Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 2.11","d":"A judge must disqualify whenever impartiality might reasonably be questioned, including personal bias, personal knowledge of disputed facts, family relationships to the third degree with a party or lawyer, economic interest, prior public statements committing the judge to a result, and prior work on the matter as lawyer, government official, witness, or judge in another court. Except for bias, the parties and lawyers may waive after disclosure, outside the judge’s presence. Unlike the ABA Model Code, North Dakota’s Rule 2.11(A) has no campaign-contribution ground, and the comment says contributions or support do not by themselves disqualify. The comment notes that district court disqualification is handled initially through N.D.C.C. § 29-15-21. A district judge who decides not to sit refers the case to the presiding judge for reassignment.","c":"N.D. Code Jud. Conduct Rule 2.11 and cmts. [1], [4]; N.D. Sup. Ct. Admin. R. 17","v":true},{"t":"Post-judgment motions stay with the trial judge","d":"A post-judgment motion or proceeding must be heard by the judge who heard the underlying matter, unless that judge cannot act, a change of judge is demanded under § 29-15-21(3) (for example in support or custody modifications), or another listed exception applies.","c":"N.D.R.Ct. 3.3","v":true}],"speech":[{"t":"No state-specific speech protection shown in the collected texts","d":"The collected statutes and rules contain no anti-SLAPP law, no statutory actual-malice rule, and no special defamation pleading rule. The North Dakota Constitution and case law were not collected, so whether its speech guarantee is read more broadly than the First Amendment was not checked.","c":"N.D. Const. (not collected)","v":false}],"other":[{"t":"Remote (“reliable electronic means”) proceedings need a timely motion","d":"In civil actions, a court may hold a hearing, conference, or other proceeding or take testimony by reliable electronic means on its own motion or a party’s. A party must first contact the others about agreement. The motion must be served at least 7 days before the proceeding, and any response at least 4 days before. A trial by remote means requires the parties’ consent and court approval.","c":"N.D.R.Ct. 11.10(b)-(c) (effective September 1, 2026)","v":true},{"t":"Continuances","d":"A party is entitled to a continuance only for the duration of a trial or hearing in which its attorney is actually engaged. Otherwise a continuance requires good cause shown promptly. A motion filed within 21 days of the trial or hearing must state whether the other side agrees, and stipulated continuances are not recognized without good cause. Requests to continue a trial must be made within 14 days after the notice of trial.","c":"N.D.R.Ct. 6.1; N.D.R.Civ.P. 40(d)","v":true}]},"appeals":{"asof":"November 10, 2026","src":"North Dakota Rules of Appellate Procedure (N.D.R.App.P.), official rule-by-rule text from ndcourts.gov (latest effective dates shown: Rules 4 and 21, 28, 30 effective September 1, 2026; Rules 3, 12, 29, 35, 40 effective July 1, 2026; Rule 10 effective April 22, 2026). N.D. Sup. Ct. Admin. R. 27 (Court of Appeals). N.D.R.Civ.P. 50, 52, 54, 58, 59, 60, 62. N.D.C.C. chs. 28-27 and 27-02.1 from ndlegis.gov. All fetched November 10, 2026.","intro":{"t":"Appeals in North Dakota: one appellate court in practice, a 60-day clock that runs from service of notice of entry","d":"Civil appeals go directly from the district court to the North Dakota Supreme Court. The notice of appeal is filed with the clerk of the Supreme Court (not the district court) within 60 days from service of notice of entry of the judgment or order. The prevailing party must serve notice of entry within 14 days after entry, but actual knowledge shown by your own affirmative action can start the clock without it.\nOnly a timely notice and payment of the docket fee go to the appeal’s validity; missing other steps is grounds for whatever action the court considers appropriate, including dismissal. The Supreme Court can extend the appeal time by up to 30 days for excusable neglect or good cause, if asked within 30 days after the deadline.\nA “temporary” Court of Appeals exists by statute through January 1, 2034, but it hears only cases the Supreme Court assigns to it, usually error-correction categories. Review from it is by discretionary petition to the Supreme Court within 14 days.","c":"N.D.R.App.P. 3(a), 4(a)(1), (a)(4); N.D.R.Civ.P. 58(b); N.D.C.C. ch. 27-02.1; N.D. Sup. Ct. Admin. R. 27"},"groups":[["Which court, and what can be appealed",[{"t":"Supreme Court is the appellate court","d":"An appeal allowed by law as of right from a district court to the Supreme Court is taken by filing a notice of appeal with the clerk of the Supreme Court. The clerk of the Supreme Court then files the notice in the district court through Odyssey. A notice mistakenly filed in district court is forwarded and treated as filed on the date the district court clerk received it.","c":"N.D.R.App.P. 3(a)(1), (d)(2), 4(f); N.D.C.C. § 28-27-01"},{"t":"Temporary Court of Appeals: assigned cases only","d":"Panels of three (retired justices, active or retired district judges, lawyers, or surrogate judges) hear only cases the Supreme Court assigns. Everything is still filed with the clerk of the Supreme Court; there are no separate filings with the Court of Appeals. Categories usually assigned include divorce property, support, and parenting appeals; misdemeanor convictions; cases from municipal court, small claims court, and administrative proceedings; mental-health commitments; certain juvenile, adoption, and parentage cases; self-represented prisoner applications; and appeals from summary-judgment orders. The Supreme Court may reassign a case to itself any time before an opinion is filed. The chapter is effective through January 1, 2034.","c":"N.D.C.C. §§ 27-02.1-01 to 27-02.1-08; N.D. Sup. Ct. Admin. R. 27, §§ 4, 9-11"},{"t":"Appealable orders","d":"By statute, a party may appeal a final judgment and these orders: an order affecting a substantial right that in effect determines the action and prevents a judgment; a final order affecting a substantial right in a special proceeding or on summary application after judgment; an order granting, refusing, continuing, or modifying a provisional remedy or injunction; an order granting or refusing a new trial or sustaining a demurrer; an order involving the merits of an action or some part of it; and an order on the frivolousness of a pleading. An order made without notice is not appealable, but an order after notice and hearing that refuses to vacate it may be.","c":"N.D.C.C. § 28-27-02"},{"t":"Partial judgments: Rule 54(b) and an extra brief section","d":"With multiple claims or parties, an order resolving fewer than all is not final unless the district court expressly determines there is no just reason for delay. The appellant’s brief must address whether the Rule 54(b) certification was appropriate, and that argument may run up to 5 pages beyond the normal page limit.","c":"N.D.R.Civ.P. 54(b); N.D.R.App.P. 28(b)(7)(C), 32(a)(8)(B)"},{"t":"Class certification orders are appealable","d":"An order certifying, refusing to certify, or amending a class certification is appealable, but the appeal does not stay district court proceedings unless the district court or Supreme Court orders.","c":"N.D.R.Civ.P. 23(d)(3), (e)(4), (t)"},{"t":"Supervisory writs and certified questions","d":"Other interlocutory review is by petition for a writ (including supervision) filed with the clerk of the Supreme Court and served on all parties, with a copy to the district judge if supervision is sought. No response is allowed unless the court asks. A district court may also certify a determinative question of law with no controlling precedent, but only after ruling on it itself; the Supreme Court may refuse a frivolous or interlocutory question.","c":"N.D.R.App.P. 21, 47.1"}]],["Notice of appeal and timing",[{"t":"60 days from service of notice of entry","d":"In a civil case the notice of appeal must be filed with the clerk of the Supreme Court within 60 days from service of notice of entry of the judgment or order. The notice must name the appellant(s), the judgment or order (or part) appealed, and the court. It must be served on all other counsel and self-represented parties, with proof of service filed with the notice. Failure to take any other step does not void the appeal but may lead to dismissal.","c":"N.D.R.App.P. 3(a), (c), (d)(1), 4(a)(1)"},{"t":"Notice of entry triggers the clock, or actual knowledge","d":"The prevailing party must serve notice of entry (with a copy of the judgment) within 14 days after entry and file it. Service of notice of entry is not needed to start the time for an appeal or post-judgment motion if the record clearly shows the appealing party’s actual knowledge of entry through its own affirmative action.","c":"N.D.R.Civ.P. 58(b)(2)-(4)"},{"t":"Extensions: up to 30 days, asked for within 30 days","d":"The Supreme Court may extend the time if a party moves no later than 30 days after the deadline and shows excusable neglect or good cause. No extension may exceed 30 days after the prescribed time. Otherwise the court cannot extend the time to appeal.","c":"N.D.R.App.P. 4(a)(4), 26(b)"},{"t":"Tolling motions","d":"A timely motion for judgment under Rule 50(b), to amend findings under Rule 52(b), for attorney’s fees under Rule 54, to alter or amend or for new trial under Rule 59, or for Rule 60 relief served and filed within 28 days after notice of entry, restarts the full appeal time for all parties from service of notice of entry of the order deciding the last such motion. If such a motion is filed after the notice of appeal, the movant must tell the Supreme Court clerk in writing, and the court may remand while keeping jurisdiction. To challenge the ruling on the motion, file a new or amended notice of appeal (no additional fee).","c":"N.D.R.App.P. 4(a)(3)"},{"t":"Cross-appeals and multiple appeals","d":"If one party timely appeals, any other party may file a notice of appeal within 14 days after the first notice was filed, or within the regular time, whichever is later. The party who files first (or the plaintiff below, if filed the same day) is the appellant.","c":"N.D.R.App.P. 1(c), 4(a)(2)"},{"t":"Docket fee","d":"The fee is paid through the electronic filing system with the notice; if payment fails, the appeal may be dismissed. Filers exempt from e-filing must pay by check or money order within 14 days of receipt or the notice is void. Where several parties file separate notices, the first to file pays. The fee amount is set by N.D.C.C. § 27-03-05, which was not collected.","c":"N.D.R.App.P. 12(b); N.D.C.C. § 27-02.1-08"},{"t":"Post-judgment mediation (family and probate cases)","d":"In divorce property or support, parenting, child support, grandparent visitation, probate code, and trust code cases, a party may request mediation within 60 days after service of notice of entry or 7 days after service of the notice of appeal. A request does not extend the appeal deadline or automatically toll briefing; transcript ordering is extended to 21 days after the notice of appeal.","c":"N.D.R.App.P. 5(d)"}]],["Record and transcript",[{"t":"Order the transcript with the notice of appeal","d":"If there was an evidentiary hearing, the appellant must order a complete transcript (unless all affected parties stipulate to portions) and file the order, and any stipulation, with the clerk of the Supreme Court with the notice of appeal. Voir dire is not transcribed unless requested. Advance payment is due within 14 days of the written estimate, or preparation is suspended.","c":"N.D.R.App.P. 10(b)(1), (c)(3)"},{"t":"Transcript and record timing","d":"The transcript is due 60 days after the order is transmitted to district court staff; the district court can extend to no more than 90 days, and further extensions come from the Supreme Court. The district court clerk forwards the record (excluding the transcript) 25 to 30 days after the notice of appeal. A statement of the evidence (when no transcript is available) must be settled by the district court and filed within 60 days after the notice of appeal.","c":"N.D.R.App.P. 10(c)(1), (d), (f), 11(a)"},{"t":"Record citation format is mandatory","d":"Cite the record by register-of-actions index number and page, for example (R156:12), or (R156:12:¶3) for a paragraph. Recordings are cited by time code (HH:MM:SS). “Id.” may not be used for record citations. Writ-petition exhibits use (E6:12).","c":"N.D.R.App.P. 21(a)(3), 30"}]],["Briefs",[{"t":"Briefing schedule","d":"Appellant’s brief: 40 days after the transcript is filed, or 40 days after the notice of appeal if no transcript is ordered. Appellee’s brief: 30 days after service of the appellant’s brief. Reply: 14 days after service of the appellee’s brief (but at least 5 days before argument if argument is less than 14 days away). An appellant who misses the deadline risks dismissal; an appellee who does not file a brief will not be heard at argument.","c":"N.D.R.App.P. 31(a), (c)"},{"t":"Page limits, not word limits","d":"A principal brief may not exceed 38 pages and a reply 12 pages, excluding any addendum, with footnotes counted. Briefs use 12-point or larger type, no more than 16 characters per inch, double spacing (footnotes too), 1.5-inch left margin, and numbered paragraphs; page numbering starts with 1 on the cover. A certificate of compliance must state the page count. Noncompliant documents will not be filed.","c":"N.D.R.App.P. 32(a)(4), (5), (7), (8), (c), (d)"},{"t":"Required contents: standard of review and preservation for each issue","d":"For each issue, the argument must state the standard of review and cite the record showing the issue was preserved (or the grounds for reviewing an unpreserved issue). Tables must reference paragraph numbers. Incorporation by reference of briefs filed elsewhere is barred. To get oral argument, put “ORAL ARGUMENT REQUESTED” on the cover of the principal or reply brief.","c":"N.D.R.App.P. 28(b), (f)(2), (h)"},{"t":"Cross-appeal briefing","d":"The appellee and cross-appellant files one combined brief when the appellee’s brief is due. The appellant answers the cross-appeal in the reply brief. The cross-appellant may file a reply limited to the cross-appeal within 14 days after service of the appellant’s reply.","c":"N.D.R.App.P. 28(i)"},{"t":"Amicus briefs","d":"Only with leave or at the court’s request, limited to issues the parties raised, no more than half a principal brief’s length, and due when the supported party’s principal brief is due. Rehearing-stage amicus briefs are limited to 10 pages and due 7 days after the petition.","c":"N.D.R.App.P. 29"},{"t":"Motions","d":"Responses are due 14 days after service of a motion; procedural motions may be decided without waiting. A motion to dismiss on the ground the appeal is not authorized by law tolls the briefing time.","c":"N.D.R.App.P. 27(b), (c), (f)"}]],["Argument, decision, and further review",[{"t":"Oral argument","d":"Argument is generally scheduled unless no party requested it, a brief was late, the transcript needed for a fact challenge was not provided, the parties waived it, or the court finds it unnecessary. The appellant gets 30 minutes (up to 10 reserved for rebuttal) and the appellee 20. A party that did not request argument must file a notice of intent to participate within 5 days of the argument notice.","c":"N.D.R.App.P. 34(a), (b)"},{"t":"Summary affirmance","d":"The court may summarily affirm by citing Rule 35.1(a) on listed grounds, including a frivolous appeal or an appellant’s brief that does not meet minimum requirements.","c":"N.D.R.App.P. 35.1"},{"t":"Rehearing: 14 days, 10 pages","d":"A petition for rehearing may be filed within 14 days after entry of judgment. It must identify each point overlooked or misapprehended. No answer is allowed unless requested, and rehearing ordinarily is not granted without such a request. Limit 10 pages. The mandate issues 21 days after entry of judgment, or 7 days after denial of a timely rehearing petition.","c":"N.D.R.App.P. 40, 41(b)"},{"t":"Review of a Court of Appeals decision","d":"Petition the Supreme Court within 14 days after the Court of Appeals judgment or order is filed, or within 14 days after it disposes of a rehearing petition (rehearing is not required first). A supporting brief of no more than 10 pages must be filed with the petition. Review is discretionary and needs a majority of the justices; listed factors include an undecided question of substance, conflict with Supreme Court or U.S. Supreme Court decisions, conflict among Court of Appeals published decisions, a dissent, or a Court of Appeals certification.","c":"N.D. Sup. Ct. Admin. R. 27, §§ 12-13; N.D.C.C. § 27-02.1-06"},{"t":"Frivolous appeals and costs","d":"For a frivolous or dilatory appeal, the court may award just damages and single or double costs, including reasonable attorney’s fees. Costs normally follow the result and include the record, the transcript, supersedeas bond premiums, and the notice-of-appeal fee, taxed in the district court.","c":"N.D.R.App.P. 38, 39"}]],["Stays and bonds",[{"t":"30-day automatic stay, then bond","d":"Execution is automatically stayed for 30 days after filing of notice of entry of judgment (not for injunctions, receiverships, or accountings). After that, a stay requires a bond or other security approved by the court. Move first in the district court; a motion in the Supreme Court must show that was impracticable or was denied. The State needs no bond.","c":"N.D.R.Civ.P. 62(a)-(c), (e); N.D.R.App.P. 8(a)"},{"t":"Cost bond","d":"The district court may require a bond or other security for costs on appeal in civil cases.","c":"N.D.R.App.P. 7(a)"}]],["E-filing and service",[{"t":"Mandatory electronic filing; 11:59 p.m. Bismarck time","d":"All documents are filed through the Supreme Court electronic filing system; self-represented litigants and prisoners are exempt. A compliant document submitted by 11:59 p.m. Bismarck time is filed that day. If a filing is rejected, time is tolled until the rejection notice, and a corrected document is timely if submitted and served within 3 days. Attorneys must accept electronic service; service is complete on transmission. Three extra days apply only to mail or commercial-carrier service; electronically served documents are treated as delivered on the service date.","c":"N.D.R.App.P. 25(a)-(b), (e), 26(c)"}]],["Deadlines",[{"t":"Notice of appeal (civil)","d":"60 days from service of notice of entry of the judgment or order.\nFiled with the clerk of the Supreme Court. Actual knowledge of entry shown by the appellant’s own affirmative action can start the clock without service (N.D.R.Civ.P. 58(b)(4)).","c":"N.D.R.App.P. 4(a)(1)","due":{"n":60,"from":"the trigger date (service of notice of entry of the judgment or order)","note":"Filed with the clerk of the Supreme Court. Actual knowledge of entry shown by the appellant’s own affirmative action can start the clock without service (N.D.R.Civ.P. 58(b)(4))."}},{"t":"Motion to extend appeal time","d":"30 days from expiration of the 60-day period.\nExcusable neglect or good cause; extension cannot exceed 30 days past the original deadline.","c":"N.D.R.App.P. 4(a)(4)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (expiration of the 60-day period)","note":"Excusable neglect or good cause; extension cannot exceed 30 days past the original deadline."}},{"t":"Cross-appeal or other party’s notice of appeal","d":"14 days from filing of the first notice of appeal (or the regular time, if later).","c":"N.D.R.App.P. 4(a)(2)","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the first notice of appeal)","note":""}},{"t":"Notice of entry of judgment by prevailing party","d":"14 days from entry of judgment.\nMust include a copy of the judgment.","c":"N.D.R.Civ.P. 58(b)(2)","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (entry of judgment)","note":"Must include a copy of the judgment."}},{"t":"Renewed JMOL; motion to amend findings; motion to alter or amend","d":"28 days from notice of entry of judgment.\nCannot be extended (N.D.R.Civ.P. 6(b)(2)).","c":"N.D.R.Civ.P. 50(b), 52(b), 59(j)","due":{"n":28,"from":"the trigger date (notice of entry of judgment)","note":"Cannot be extended (N.D.R.Civ.P. 6(b)(2))."}},{"t":"Motion for new trial (other than newly discovered evidence)","d":"60 days from notice of entry of judgment.\nThe court may extend for good cause. Six months for newly discovered evidence. Rule 4(a)(3)(A) counts a new-trial motion filed within the time the civil rules allow, so a motion filed within this period appears to restart the appeal time; no case law on this point was researched.","c":"N.D.R.Civ.P. 59(c)(2)","due":{"n":60,"from":"the trigger date (notice of entry of judgment)","note":"The court may extend for good cause. Six months for newly discovered evidence. Rule 4(a)(3)(A) counts a new-trial motion filed within the time the civil rules allow, so a motion filed within this period appears to restart the appeal time; no case law on this point was researched."}},{"t":"Rule 60 motion that tolls appeal time","d":"28 days from notice of entry of judgment.","c":"N.D.R.App.P. 4(a)(3)(A)(vi)","due":{"n":28,"from":"the trigger date (notice of entry of judgment)","note":""}},{"t":"Attorney’s fees motion","d":"21 days from notice of entry of judgment.\nA timely fee motion is a tolling motion under N.D.R.App.P. 4(a)(3)(A)(iii).","c":"N.D.R.Civ.P. 54(e)(2)(A)","due":{"n":21,"from":"the trigger date (notice of entry of judgment)","note":"A timely fee motion is a tolling motion under N.D.R.App.P. 4(a)(3)(A)(iii)."}},{"t":"Transcript order","d":"0 days from filed with the notice of appeal.\n21 days after the notice of appeal in cases assigned to post-judgment mediation (N.D.R.App.P. 5(d)(6)).","c":"N.D.R.App.P. 10(b)(1)(E)","due":{"n":0,"from":"the trigger date (filed with the notice of appeal)","note":"21 days after the notice of appeal in cases assigned to post-judgment mediation (N.D.R.App.P. 5(d)(6))."}},{"t":"Transcript advance payment","d":"14 days from written cost estimate.\nPreparation is suspended if not paid.","c":"N.D.R.App.P. 10(c)(3)","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (written cost estimate)","note":"Preparation is suspended if not paid."}},{"t":"Transcript filed","d":"60 days from transmission of the transcript order to district court staff.\nDistrict court may extend to no more than 90 days total.","c":"N.D.R.App.P. 10(c)(1), (d)(2)","due":{"n":60,"from":"the trigger date (transmission of the transcript order to district court staff)","note":"District court may extend to no more than 90 days total."}},{"t":"Appellant’s brief","d":"40 days from filing of the transcript (or of the notice of appeal if no transcript).\n38-page limit.","c":"N.D.R.App.P. 31(a)","due":{"n":40,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the transcript)","note":"38-page limit."}},{"t":"Appellee’s brief","d":"30 days from service of the appellant’s brief.","c":"N.D.R.App.P. 31(a)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (service of the appellant’s brief)","note":""}},{"t":"Reply brief","d":"14 days from service of the appellee’s brief.\nMust be filed at least 5 days before argument if argument is less than 14 days away; 12-page limit.","c":"N.D.R.App.P. 31(a)","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (service of the appellee’s brief)","note":"Must be filed at least 5 days before argument if argument is less than 14 days away; 12-page limit."}},{"t":"Response to a motion","d":"14 days from service of the motion.","c":"N.D.R.App.P. 27(b)","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (service of the motion)","note":""}},{"t":"Notice of intent to participate in oral argument","d":"5 days from service of the notice of oral argument.\nOnly for a party that did not request argument.","c":"N.D.R.App.P. 34(a)(3)","due":{"n":5,"from":"the trigger date (service of the notice of oral argument)","note":"Only for a party that did not request argument."}},{"t":"Petition for rehearing","d":"14 days from entry of judgment.\n10-page limit.","c":"N.D.R.App.P. 40(a)(1)","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (entry of judgment)","note":"10-page limit."}},{"t":"Mandate issues","d":"21 days from entry of judgment (or 7 days after denial of rehearing).","c":"N.D.R.App.P. 41(b)","due":{"n":21,"from":"the trigger date (entry of judgment)","note":""}},{"t":"Petition for Supreme Court review of a Court of Appeals decision","d":"14 days from filing of the Court of Appeals judgment or order (or disposition of rehearing).\nSupporting brief of no more than 10 pages filed with the petition.","c":"N.D. Sup. Ct. Admin. R. 27, § 13(a)","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the Court of Appeals judgment or order)","note":"Supporting brief of no more than 10 pages filed with the petition."}}]]],"courts":[],"links":[["Official site","https://www.ndcourts.gov/"]]},"rulesName":"North Dakota Rules of Civil Procedure","rulesSrc":"https://www.ndcourts.gov/legal-resources/rules/ndrcivp","rulesAsof":"as checked November 10, 2026","rulesNote":"Each entry is our summary of the rule, not the rule itself. Tap Read the official text before you rely on it.","rules":[{"n":"1","t":"Scope of rules","s":"The rules govern all civil actions and proceedings in district court, except as stated in Rule 81, and are to be construed for the just, speedy, and inexpensive determination of every action. Unlike FRCP 1, they apply in state district court (North Dakota has one level of general trial court).","u":"https://www.ndcourts.gov/legal-resources/rules/ndrcivp"},{"n":"2","t":"One form of action","s":"There is one form of action, the civil action.","u":"https://www.ndcourts.gov/legal-resources/rules/ndrcivp"},{"n":"3","t":"Commencing an action","s":"A civil action is commenced by service of a summons, not by filing. This is the key difference from FRCP 3: North Dakota allows “pocket” service, and the case may exist for some time before anything is filed (see Rule 5(d)(2)).","u":"https://www.ndcourts.gov/legal-resources/rules/ndrcivp"},{"n":"4","t":"Persons subject to jurisdiction; process; service","s":"Rule 4(b) is North Dakota’s long-arm provision (jurisdiction over persons found or domiciled in the state, plus contact-based jurisdiction for listed acts such as transacting business or committing a tort causing in-state injury, to the limits of due process), and allows a stay or dismissal for inconvenient forum. The summons must state the time to answer and warn of default, and is served with the complaint (except by publication). Personal service may be by delivery, abode service, delivery to an agent, or any mail or commercial delivery requiring a signed receipt and resulting in delivery; a refused restricted delivery counts as delivery (Rule 4(l)). Publication requires a filed complaint and declaration, three weekly publications, mailing within 14 days after first publication, and first publication or out-of-state service within 60 days of the declaration or the action is discontinued as to that defendant; publication service is complete 15 days after first publication, and a defendant served by publication without actual notice may move to defend up to three years after judgment (not in divorce). Unlike FRCP 4(m), the rule sets no 90-day service deadline, because filing comes after service.","u":"https://www.ndcourts.gov/legal-resources/rules/ndrcivp"},{"n":"5","t":"Service and filing of pleadings and other documents","s":"Documents required to be filed must be served electronically under N.D.R.Ct. 3.5 to the attorney’s designated e-service address posted on the Supreme Court website; electronic service is complete on transmission but ineffective if the sender learns it did not arrive. All documents are filed electronically through Odyssey. The initiating pleading must be filed with proof of Rule 4 service, and the filer must serve notice of filing. A defendant may serve a demand that the plaintiff file: if the complaint is not filed within 20 days after service of the demand, service of the summons is void (unless, on motion within 60 days after the demand, the court finds excusable neglect); the defendant may instead file the papers itself. Discovery materials are not filed except for a motion, by order, or for certified safekeeping. Hearing declarations and documents must be filed at least 24 hours before the hearing. The clerk must reject a filing that adds a party without a court order, except as allowed by Rules 13, 14, or 15.","u":"https://www.ndcourts.gov/legal-resources/rules/ndrcivp"},{"n":"6","t":"Computing and extending time; time for motion papers","s":"Time is computed as under FRCP 6(a), with an electronic-filing day ending at midnight in the court’s time zone and “legal holiday” defined by N.D.C.C. §§ 1-03-01, 1-03-02, and 1-03-02.1. The court may extend time for good cause, but not the times under Rules 4(e)(7), 50(b) and (d), 52(b), 59(i) and (j), and 60(b). If an evidentiary hearing is requested, the motion and notice must be served at least 21 days before the hearing (general motion practice is in N.D.R.Ct. 3.2). Three days are added after service by mail or commercial carrier under Rule 5; for Rule 4 mail service the period runs from delivery.","u":"https://www.ndcourts.gov/legal-resources/rules/ndrcivp"},{"n":"7","t":"Pleadings allowed; form of motions","s":"Allowed pleadings are a complaint, answer, answer to a counterclaim or crossclaim, third-party complaint and answer, and a reply only if the court orders one. A motion must be in writing (unless made at a hearing or trial), state the grounds with particularity, and state the relief sought.","u":"https://www.ndcourts.gov/legal-resources/rules/ndrcivp"},{"n":"8","t":"General rules of pleading","s":"Notice pleading: a short and plain statement showing entitlement to relief and a demand for relief. Denials, general denials, and lack-of-knowledge responses work as in FRCP 8; an allegation other than damages is admitted if not denied. The list of affirmative defenses that must be pleaded tracks the pre-2010 federal list (it still includes “discharge in bankruptcy”).","u":"https://www.ndcourts.gov/legal-resources/rules/ndrcivp"},{"n":"9","t":"Pleading special matters","s":"Fraud or mistake must be pleaded with particularity; malice, intent, and knowledge may be alleged generally. Denial of a condition precedent must be particular. Special damages must be specifically stated. Rule 9(h) allows an unknown party to be designated by any name and amended later (a North Dakota addition).","u":"https://www.ndcourts.gov/legal-resources/rules/ndrcivp"},{"n":"10","t":"Form of pleadings","s":"The caption must give the court’s name and the county, the parties, and the Rule 7(a) designation. Claims go in numbered paragraphs. If the State of North Dakota is a real party in interest but not named, later pleadings must name the State in the title until it files a notice that it is no longer a real party in interest. A document filed with a pleading is part of it for all purposes.","u":"https://www.ndcourts.gov/legal-resources/rules/ndrcivp"},{"n":"11","t":"Signing; representations to court; sanctions","s":"Every paper must be signed and must give the signer’s address, e-mail address for electronic service, telephone number, and (for an attorney) State Board of Law Examiners ID number. Notarization is generally not required, and an unsworn declaration may replace an affidavit. The certification and sanctions provisions track FRCP 11, including the 21-day safe harbor; the respondent to a sanctions motion has 10 days after filing to serve and file an answer brief. Unlike the federal rule, a factual contention lacking immediate evidentiary support need not be specifically identified. Former Rule 11(e) on limited representation moved to N.D.R.Ct. 11.9 effective March 1, 2024 (Rule 5(b)(2)(A) still cross-refers to “Rule 11(e)”).","u":"https://www.ndcourts.gov/legal-resources/rules/ndrcivp"},{"n":"12","t":"Defenses and objections","s":"Answer due 21 days after service of the summons and complaint (21 days for a counterclaim or crossclaim answer; 21 days after an order to reply). A denied or postponed Rule 12 motion resets the answer to 14 days after notice of the court’s action; a more definite statement must be served within 14 days after the order. The seven Rule 12(b) defenses, waiver rules, and the conversion of a 12(b)(6) or 12(c) motion to summary judgment track FRCP 12. A motion to strike must be made before responding or within 21 days after service if no response is allowed.","u":"https://www.ndcourts.gov/legal-resources/rules/ndrcivp"},{"n":"13","t":"Counterclaim and crossclaim","s":"Compulsory counterclaims (same transaction or occurrence) must be pleaded, as in FRCP 13. In a contract action for money only, if the answer does not deny the claim but asserts a smaller counterclaim, the plaintiff may take default judgment for the excess (Rule 13(c)(2), a North Dakota addition). The rules do not expand counterclaims against the State.","u":"https://www.ndcourts.gov/legal-resources/rules/ndrcivp"},{"n":"14","t":"Third-party practice","s":"A defending party may implead a nonparty who may be liable to it, without leave if the third-party summons and complaint are served within 14 days after serving its original answer, and otherwise by motion. A plaintiff facing a counterclaim may also bring in a third party. Admiralty provisions of the federal rule are omitted.","u":"https://www.ndcourts.gov/legal-resources/rules/ndrcivp"},{"n":"15","t":"Amended and supplemental pleadings","s":"A party may amend once as a matter of course before being served with a responsive pleading, or within 21 days after serving a pleading to which no response is allowed if the case is not on the trial calendar. This differs from FRCP 15(a)(1), which allows amendment within 21 days after service of a responsive pleading or Rule 12 motion. Otherwise amendment requires written consent or leave, freely given. Response to an amended pleading is due within the time left to answer the original or 14 days after service, whichever is later. Relation back tracks FRCP 15(c); notice to the State is satisfied by delivery or mailing of process to the Attorney General or the officer or agency.","u":"https://www.ndcourts.gov/legal-resources/rules/ndrcivp"},{"n":"16","t":"Pretrial conferences; scheduling; management","s":"The court must hold a scheduling conference, within 60 days of the triggering event, if: more than six months pass after filing without final disposition or a dispositive motion; the case was served more than six months before filing and 90 days have passed since filing; a Rule 40(e) notice draws a request to keep the case open; or any party asks in writing. Conferences may be by phone or electronic means. Sanctions track FRCP 16(f), with mandatory expenses absent substantial justification.","u":"https://www.ndcourts.gov/legal-resources/rules/ndrcivp"},{"n":"17","t":"Plaintiff and defendant; capacity","s":"Actions must be brought by the real party in interest, with time to ratify, join, or substitute before dismissal. Minors and incompetent persons sue through a representative, next friend, or guardian ad litem. Capacity of other persons is set by statute (no FRCP 17(b) choice-of-law rule).","u":"https://www.ndcourts.gov/legal-resources/rules/ndrcivp"},{"n":"18","t":"Joinder of claims and remedies","s":"A party may join as many claims as it has. A liability or indemnity insurer may not be joined in a tort case unless directly liable by law or contract to the injured person (a North Dakota addition).","u":"https://www.ndcourts.gov/legal-resources/rules/ndrcivp"},{"n":"19","t":"Required joinder of parties","s":"Tracks FRCP 19: persons needed for complete relief or with interests at risk must be joined if feasible; otherwise the court weighs the listed factors in equity and good conscience. A joined party whose joinder makes venue improper must be dismissed if it objects.","u":"https://www.ndcourts.gov/legal-resources/rules/ndrcivp"},{"n":"20","t":"Permissive joinder of parties","s":"Plaintiffs or defendants may be joined if the claims arise from the same transaction, occurrence, or series and share a common question of law or fact. The court may order separate trials to prevent prejudice.","u":"https://www.ndcourts.gov/legal-resources/rules/ndrcivp"},{"n":"21","t":"Misjoinder and nonjoinder of parties","s":"Misjoinder is not a ground for dismissal; the court may add or drop parties or sever claims at any time on just terms.","u":"https://www.ndcourts.gov/legal-resources/rules/ndrcivp"},{"n":"22","t":"Interpleader","s":"A plaintiff exposed to multiple liability may join claimants and require them to interplead; a defendant may do so by crossclaim or counterclaim.","u":"https://www.ndcourts.gov/legal-resources/rules/ndrcivp"},{"n":"23","t":"Class actions","s":"North Dakota uses the Uniform Class Actions Rule, not FRCP 23. A class action requires numerosity (or impracticable joinder) and a common question; the court then decides whether a class action is fair and efficient under 13 listed factors and whether the representatives (and counsel, and their finances) are adequate. Orders certifying, refusing to certify, or amending certification are appealable, but an appeal does not stay district court proceedings unless the district court or Supreme Court orders. Notice must go to the class after certification; members with a potential recovery or liability over $100 must get personal, mailed, or commercial-carrier notice if they can be found. Plaintiff-class members may opt out (with listed exceptions); defendant-class members may not. The statute of limitations is tolled for all class members when the class action is commenced. The rule has detailed provisions on counterclaims, dismissal or compromise (court approval after hearing), undistributed funds (to states as unclaimed property or to the defendant), and attorney’s fees.","u":"https://www.ndcourts.gov/legal-resources/rules/ndrcivp"},{"n":"24","t":"Intervention","s":"Intervention of right and permissive intervention track FRCP 24, on timely motion with a pleading attached. A party challenging the constitutionality of a legislative act affecting the public interest, when the State is not a party, must notify the Attorney General, who must be allowed to intervene.","u":"https://www.ndcourts.gov/legal-resources/rules/ndrcivp"},{"n":"25","t":"Substitution of parties","s":"After a party’s death, a motion to substitute must be made within 90 days after service of a statement noting the death, or the action may be dismissed. The motion is served on parties under Rule 5 and on nonparties under Rule 4. Incompetency, transfer of interest, and public-officer succession track FRCP 25.","u":"https://www.ndcourts.gov/legal-resources/rules/ndrcivp"},{"n":"26","t":"General provisions governing discovery","s":"North Dakota has no FRCP 26(a) initial, expert-report, or pretrial disclosures. Scope is any nonprivileged matter relevant to any party’s claim or defense (broader subject-matter discovery for good cause), and information need not be admissible if reasonably calculated to lead to admissible evidence, a standard the federal rule dropped in 2015. Proportionality appears as a mandatory court limit. “Electronically stored information” includes reasonably accessible metadata showing date sent, date received, author, and recipients, but not other metadata absent agreement or good cause. Expert discovery is by interrogatory (identity, subject, substance, grounds) and deposition, not written reports. Any party may request a discovery meeting no earlier than 40 days after the complaint is filed; the meeting must occur within 21 days of the request, and a discovery plan or joint report is due 14 days after the meeting. Discovery papers must be signed and include the signer’s ID number.","u":"https://www.ndcourts.gov/legal-resources/rules/ndrcivp"},{"n":"27","t":"Depositions before action or pending appeal","s":"A verified petition to perpetuate testimony is filed in the county where an expected opposing party resides, with at least 21 days’ notice before the hearing. Depositions pending appeal require a motion in the trial court.","u":"https://www.ndcourts.gov/legal-resources/rules/ndrcivp"},{"n":"28","t":"Persons before whom depositions may be taken","s":"Depositions in the United States are taken before an officer authorized by North Dakota, federal, or local law, or a court-appointed person; abroad, under treaty, letter of request, notice, or commission. A party’s relative, employee, or attorney (or someone financially interested) may not serve as officer.","u":"https://www.ndcourts.gov/legal-resources/rules/ndrcivp"},{"n":"29","t":"Stipulations about discovery procedure","s":"Parties may stipulate in writing to deposition logistics and other discovery procedures, but an extension that would interfere with the time set for completing discovery, a motion hearing, or trial needs court approval.","u":"https://www.ndcourts.gov/legal-resources/rules/ndrcivp"},{"n":"30","t":"Depositions by oral examination","s":"The plaintiff needs leave to take a deposition less than 30 days after serving a defendant, unless the defendant has sought discovery or a special notice says the deponent is about to leave the state. Unlike FRCP 30, there is no 10-deposition limit and no 7-hour limit in the text. Testimony may be recorded by audio, audiovisual, or stenographic means; remote depositions need a stipulation or order. Rule 30(b)(6) organizational depositions require a good-faith conference on the topics. Objections must be concise and nonargumentative; instructions not to answer are limited as in the federal rule. The deponent has 30 days after notice that the transcript is available to review and sign if review was requested.","u":"https://www.ndcourts.gov/legal-resources/rules/ndrcivp"},{"n":"30.1","t":"Uniform audio-visual deposition rule","s":"Repealed.","u":"https://www.ndcourts.gov/legal-resources/rules/ndrcivp"},{"n":"31","t":"Depositions by written questions","s":"Cross-questions are due 14 days after service of the notice and direct questions; redirect 7 days after cross; recross 7 days after redirect. Leave is needed only if the deponent is in prison.","u":"https://www.ndcourts.gov/legal-resources/rules/ndrcivp"},{"n":"32","t":"Using depositions in court proceedings","s":"Depositions may be used against a party who was present, represented, or had notice. A witness’s deposition may be used for any purpose if the witness is dead, more than 100 miles from the place of hearing or trial or outside the state, unable to attend, or cannot be subpoenaed, or in exceptional circumstances. A deposition may not be used against a party who had less than 14 days’ notice and promptly moved for a protective order that was still pending. Objections to written-question form are waived unless served within the time for responsive questions (seven days for recross).","u":"https://www.ndcourts.gov/legal-resources/rules/ndrcivp"},{"n":"33","t":"Interrogatories to parties","s":"Limit of 50 interrogatories per party served on any other party (FRCP allows 25), with logically subsumed subparts not counted separately. Answers and objections are due in 30 days, but a defendant need not respond until 45 days after service of the summons and complaint. Each answer must restate the interrogatory. Unstated objections are waived absent good cause. An interrogatory served by one party is considered served by all.","u":"https://www.ndcourts.gov/legal-resources/rules/ndrcivp"},{"n":"34","t":"Producing documents, ESI, and tangible things; entry on land","s":"Responses are due within 30 days after service, but a defendant need not respond until 45 days after service of the summons and complaint. Objections must state whether responsive materials are being withheld. ESI is produced in the requested form, the form ordinarily maintained, or a reasonably usable form. Nonparties are reached by Rule 45 subpoena.","u":"https://www.ndcourts.gov/legal-resources/rules/ndrcivp"},{"n":"35","t":"Physical and mental examination","s":"On motion for good cause, the court may order a party whose condition is in controversy to be examined by a suitably licensed or certified examiner. Report exchange and privilege waiver track FRCP 35.","u":"https://www.ndcourts.gov/legal-resources/rules/ndrcivp"},{"n":"36","t":"Requests for admission","s":"A matter is admitted unless a written answer or objection is served within 30 days, but a defendant need not respond until 45 days after service of the summons and complaint. Admitted matters are conclusively established unless the court allows withdrawal or amendment.","u":"https://www.ndcourts.gov/legal-resources/rules/ndrcivp"},{"n":"37","t":"Failure to make or cooperate in discovery; sanctions","s":"A motion to compel must certify a good-faith attempt to confer. Expense-shifting and sanction tiers track FRCP 37, but North Dakota has no counterparts to FRCP 37(c)(1) (failure to disclose) because there are no initial disclosures. Expenses and fees may not be awarded against the State except as allowed by statute. Rule 37(f) protects ESI lost through routine, good-faith operation of a system, absent exceptional circumstances (the pre-2015 federal safe harbor rather than current FRCP 37(e)). Failure to participate in good faith in a Rule 26(f) discovery plan may draw expenses.","u":"https://www.ndcourts.gov/legal-resources/rules/ndrcivp"},{"n":"38","t":"Right to a jury trial; demand","s":"A jury demand must be served no later than 14 days after the last pleading directed to the issue and filed under Rule 5(d). The jury is six unless a party specifically demands nine within the same time (FRCP 38 has no such election). Failure to serve and file a proper demand waives the jury.","u":"https://www.ndcourts.gov/legal-resources/rules/ndrcivp"},{"n":"39","t":"Trial by jury or by the court","s":"A demanded jury trial proceeds unless the parties stipulate otherwise or the court finds no jury right. The court may order a jury on motion even without a demand, use an advisory jury, or, with consent, a binding jury.","u":"https://www.ndcourts.gov/legal-resources/rules/ndrcivp"},{"n":"39.1","t":"Change in location; change of venue","s":"The court may move a hearing or trial to another county whose seat is less than 10 miles from the venue county’s seat, for impartiality, or for convenience and efficiency; any party may object within 14 days after notice of the new location, and the original venue then controls unless grounds for a venue change exist. Venue may be changed for improper county, impartiality, or convenience of witnesses and the ends of justice; a change to another judicial district requires a new judge. No federal counterpart.","u":"https://www.ndcourts.gov/legal-resources/rules/ndrcivp"},{"n":"40","t":"Assignment of cases for trial","s":"District courts are in continuous session in each county. A request to continue a trial must be made within 14 days after receiving the notice of trial (waivable for unavoidable circumstances). The court may dismiss without prejudice a filed action with no prosecution for more than one year.","u":"https://www.ndcourts.gov/legal-resources/rules/ndrcivp"},{"n":"41","t":"Dismissal of actions","s":"A plaintiff may dismiss without order by notice before an answer or summary-judgment motion is served (not when a provisional remedy has been allowed), or by stipulation; a second notice dismissal of the same claim is an adjudication on the merits. Other voluntary dismissals require a court order. Involuntary dismissals generally operate as adjudications on the merits. The court may order costs of a previously dismissed action.","u":"https://www.ndcourts.gov/legal-resources/rules/ndrcivp"},{"n":"42","t":"Consolidation; separate trials","s":"Actions with common questions may be consolidated or jointly heard; separate trials may be ordered for convenience or to avoid prejudice, preserving any jury right.","u":"https://www.ndcourts.gov/legal-resources/rules/ndrcivp"},{"n":"43","t":"Evidence","s":"Testimony is taken in open court, but for good cause or by agreement the court may allow testimony by contemporaneous transmission from another location; notice is required. Motions may be heard on declarations. The court must provide an interpreter for a party, witness, or other significant participant with limited English proficiency or who is deaf.","u":"https://www.ndcourts.gov/legal-resources/rules/ndrcivp"},{"n":"44","t":"Proving an official record","s":"Domestic and foreign official records are proved by official publication or attested copy with the required certification; lack of a record by a diligent-search statement.","u":"https://www.ndcourts.gov/legal-resources/rules/ndrcivp"},{"n":"44.1","t":"Determining foreign law","s":"A party raising foreign law must give notice in a pleading or writing; the court may consider any relevant source, and its determination is a ruling on law.","u":"https://www.ndcourts.gov/legal-resources/rules/ndrcivp"},{"n":"45","t":"Subpoena","s":"The clerk issues subpoenas signed but blank, and a North Dakota-licensed attorney may issue one. Subpoenas are served under Rule 4(d) anywhere in the state, with one day’s fees and mileage tendered (or the witness need not obey). Before serving a subpoena for a pretrial deposition or pretrial production, a notice of deposition or notice of demand for production must be served on every party. A records subpoena must carry the Rule 45(f) notice; written objections must be received before the earlier of 24 hours before compliance or 10 days after service, and a timely objection excuses compliance until the court orders it. A resident can be required to attend a deposition only in the county where the person resides, works, or does business in person, or where the court orders. Out-of-state actions use N.D.R.Ct. 5.1.","u":"https://www.ndcourts.gov/legal-resources/rules/ndrcivp"},{"n":"46","t":"Objecting to a ruling or order","s":"Formal exceptions are unnecessary; a party need only state the action wanted or the objection and grounds.","u":"https://www.ndcourts.gov/legal-resources/rules/ndrcivp"},{"n":"47","t":"Jurors","s":"The court calls only as many prospective jurors as needed to seat the jury plus the available peremptory challenges, unless otherwise stipulated and approved. The parties have the right to examine jurors themselves. Each side gets four peremptory challenges regardless of jury size or number of parties (more if co-parties are adverse), exercised alternately starting with the plaintiff, plus one more if alternates are called (usable only on alternates). One or two alternates may be seated and retained during deliberations.","u":"https://www.ndcourts.gov/legal-resources/rules/ndrcivp"},{"n":"48","t":"Number of jurors; verdict","s":"Civil juries have six jurors unless a party demands nine under Rule 38. Parties may stipulate to fewer than nine or to a stated majority verdict. Polling is on request.","u":"https://www.ndcourts.gov/legal-resources/rules/ndrcivp"},{"n":"49","t":"Special verdict; general verdict and questions","s":"Tracks FRCP 49: special verdicts, general verdicts with written questions, and handling of inconsistencies.","u":"https://www.ndcourts.gov/legal-resources/rules/ndrcivp"},{"n":"50","t":"Judgment as a matter of law; new trial; conditional ruling","s":"A JMOL motion may be made any time before submission to the jury. A renewed motion must be served and filed no later than 28 days after notice of entry of judgment (FRCP 50(b) runs from entry of judgment), or 28 days after discharge if a jury issue was undecided. A new-trial motion by a party against whom JMOL is entered is due 28 days after notice of entry. These times cannot be extended (Rule 6(b)(2)).","u":"https://www.ndcourts.gov/legal-resources/rules/ndrcivp"},{"n":"51","t":"Instructions to jury","s":"Requests are filed at the close of evidence or earlier as ordered, and may cite North Dakota pattern instructions by number. The court must disclose proposed instructions and allow objections on the record outside the jury’s hearing before instructing and before final arguments. Final instructions must be in writing unless the parties agree otherwise. Unpreserved error is reviewable only as plain error. Preliminary instructions may cover juror written questions under N.D.R.Ct. 6.8.","u":"https://www.ndcourts.gov/legal-resources/rules/ndrcivp"},{"n":"52","t":"Findings and conclusions in a nonjury proceeding","s":"In bench trials the court must find facts specially and state conclusions separately; findings are reviewed for clear error. No findings are required on Rule 12 or 56 motions. A motion to amend or add findings is due no later than 28 days after notice of entry of judgment and cannot be extended. Judgment on partial findings is allowed.","u":"https://www.ndcourts.gov/legal-resources/rules/ndrcivp"},{"n":"53","t":"Masters","s":"A master may be appointed by consent, for bench-trial issues on exceptional conditions or complex accounting, or for pretrial and post-trial matters a judge cannot timely address. The disqualification cross-reference is to Canon 3(E) of the Code of Judicial Conduct. Objections to a master’s order, report, or recommendations are due 21 days after service. In eminent-domain cases where a jury is waived, the court may appoint one or three masters to set compensation.","u":"https://www.ndcourts.gov/legal-resources/rules/ndrcivp"},{"n":"54","t":"Judgment; costs","s":"Rule 54(b) partial final judgment requires an express determination of no just reason for delay, as in FRCP 54(b). Costs and disbursements are allowed by statute: the party awarded costs must serve and file a detailed, verified statement within 30 days after entry of the order for judgment; objections are due 14 days after service; a hearing may be requested within 7 days of filing objections. A motion for attorney’s fees not determined by the judgment must be served and filed within 21 days after notice of entry of judgment, and may be decided after an appeal is filed.","u":"https://www.ndcourts.gov/legal-resources/rules/ndrcivp"},{"n":"55","t":"Default; default judgment","s":"There is no separate clerk’s entry of default; the court may direct entry of default judgment. For a sum certain, judgment may be entered on a declaration of the amount due and the written instrument; otherwise the court takes proof. A party who has appeared must be served with the motion for default judgment and notice complying with N.D.R.Ct. 3.2(a). After publication or out-of-state service, the court may require a restitution bond before default judgment (not in title or lien-foreclosure cases).","u":"https://www.ndcourts.gov/legal-resources/rules/ndrcivp"},{"n":"56","t":"Summary judgment","s":"A claimant may move after 21 days from commencement or after the opponent moves; a defending party may move at any time. The motion and supporting documents must be filed at least 90 days before trial and 45 days before the hearing unless otherwise ordered. The opposing party has 30 days after service of the brief to serve and file an answer brief; the reply is due 14 days later. Principal and answer briefs are limited to 38 pages and replies to 12 pages (footnotes counted), 12-point type, double-spaced; a request to exceed the limit (two pages maximum) must be filed at least 7 days before the brief is due. The text uses the older “no genuine issue as to any material fact” formulation and Rule 56(f) for declarations that facts are unavailable.","u":"https://www.ndcourts.gov/legal-resources/rules/ndrcivp"},{"n":"57","t":"Declaratory judgments","s":"Procedure for declaratory judgments under N.D.C.C. ch. 32-23; jury rights under Rules 38 and 39; another adequate remedy does not bar declaratory relief.","u":"https://www.ndcourts.gov/legal-resources/rules/ndrcivp"},{"n":"58","t":"Entry and notice of entry of judgment","s":"After an order for judgment is filed, the prevailing party submits a form of judgment, which becomes effective when the clerk signs, files, and enters it; if the prevailing party does not submit one within 30 days, any party may. The prevailing party must serve notice of entry of judgment (with a copy of the judgment) within 14 days after entry, and file the notice. Service of notice of entry is what starts most post-judgment and appeal deadlines, unless the record shows the moving or appealing party’s actual knowledge of entry through its own affirmative action. No FRCP counterpart to this notice requirement.","u":"https://www.ndcourts.gov/legal-resources/rules/ndrcivp"},{"n":"59","t":"New trial; amending a judgment","s":"Grounds are listed by statute-style categories (irregularity, juror misconduct, surprise, newly discovered evidence, excessive damages from passion or prejudice with remittitur, insufficient evidence, legal error, inability to obtain a transcript). A new-trial motion must be served and filed within six months after notice of entry of judgment if based on newly discovered evidence, and within 60 days on any other ground unless the court extends the time for good cause, much longer than FRCP 59’s 28 days. The court must file a memorandum stating its grounds. The court’s own new-trial order is due no later than 28 days after notice of entry. A motion to alter or amend is due no later than 28 days after notice of entry of judgment. Rule 59(i) and (j) times cannot be extended.","u":"https://www.ndcourts.gov/legal-resources/rules/ndrcivp"},{"n":"60","t":"Relief from a judgment or order","s":"Amended effective July 1, 2026. Clerical mistakes and oversights may be corrected at any time, but only with the Supreme Court’s leave while an appeal is docketed and pending. Rule 60(b) grounds track the federal rule; a motion must be made within a reasonable time, and for mistake, newly discovered evidence, or fraud no more than one year after notice of entry of the judgment or order if the opposing party appeared, or one year after entry of a default judgment. Leave of the appellate court is needed only if an appeal is actually pending. The rule also applies to municipal courts.","u":"https://www.ndcourts.gov/legal-resources/rules/ndrcivp"},{"n":"61","t":"Harmless error","s":"Errors not affecting substantial rights are disregarded.","u":"https://www.ndcourts.gov/legal-resources/rules/ndrcivp"},{"n":"62","t":"Stay of proceedings to enforce a judgment","s":"Execution is automatically stayed for 30 days after filing of notice of entry of judgment or entry of a default judgment, unless ordered otherwise; injunctions, receiverships, and accountings are not stayed. A party may obtain a stay at any time by bond or other security approved by the court. The State and its agencies need no bond. Judgments for delivery of property, conveyances, sale or possession of real property, abatement of nuisance, and other specific acts require specific undertakings (generally with at least two sureties) to stay execution on appeal. An order staying proceedings for more than 21 days requires prior notice, except a stay under an order or judgment appealed from.","u":"https://www.ndcourts.gov/legal-resources/rules/ndrcivp"},{"n":"63","t":"Judge’s inability to proceed","s":"A successor judge may proceed after certifying familiarity with the record and must, on request in a hearing or bench trial, recall material disputed witnesses who are available.","u":"https://www.ndcourts.gov/legal-resources/rules/ndrcivp"},{"n":"64","t":"Seizing property","s":"All state-law remedies for seizing property to secure a potential judgment are available at the start of and throughout the action.","u":"https://www.ndcourts.gov/legal-resources/rules/ndrcivp"},{"n":"65","t":"Injunctions","s":"Rewritten North Dakota rule. A TRO motion must be submitted with a proposed complaint and filed by the next court business day or the TRO terminates. The movant must declare its notice efforts. The preliminary-injunction hearing must be set no less than 21 and no more than 28 days after the TRO, and the TRO expires at the end of the 28th day unless extended (up to 14 more days after the hearing if needed to decide). An opposing party with less than four days’ actual notice may move to dissolve on four days’ notice. With a TRO in place, the movant must serve the PI motion and brief within 7 days after the TRO, and the response is due 7 days after service; without a TRO, the response is due 7 days after service and the reply 5 days after that. PI motion papers must be served at least 14 days before the hearing. If a PI issues, trial must be held within 180 days of the first TRO or PI and a decision should follow within 60 days of trial. The motion must disclose any prior denial by another judge. Security is required (government exempt) and does not cap damages.","u":"https://www.ndcourts.gov/legal-resources/rules/ndrcivp"},{"n":"66","t":"Receivers","s":"Administration of receiverships follows statute or local rule. A foreign receiver may sue but is subordinate to local creditors. Dismissal requires a court order.","u":"https://www.ndcourts.gov/legal-resources/rules/ndrcivp"},{"n":"67","t":"Deposit into court","s":"With leave and notice, a party may deposit money or a thing in dispute; funds are handled under N.D.C.C. ch. 32-11 and may be placed in insured interest-bearing accounts.","u":"https://www.ndcourts.gov/legal-resources/rules/ndrcivp"},{"n":"68","t":"Offer of settlement or confession of judgment; tender","s":"Either party (not only a defending party, as in FRCP 68) may serve an offer of settlement at least 14 days before trial; acceptance is due within 14 days. If the offeree’s final judgment is not more favorable, the offeree pays costs incurred after the offer. After liability is decided, an offer may be served at least 7 days before the damages hearing. In money actions, a party may instead tender the full amount owed plus accrued costs at least 14 days before trial; if not accepted within 14 days, the offeree recovers no costs unless it recovers more. Rule 68(c) governs confession of judgment without action by verified statement.","u":"https://www.ndcourts.gov/legal-resources/rules/ndrcivp"},{"n":"69","t":"Execution","s":"Money judgments are enforced by writ of execution under state statutes; post-judgment discovery is available under the rules.","u":"https://www.ndcourts.gov/legal-resources/rules/ndrcivp"},{"n":"70","t":"Enforcing a judgment for a specific act","s":"The court may appoint someone to perform the act at the disobedient party’s expense, vest title by judgment, issue writs of attachment, sequestration, execution, or assistance, or hold the party in contempt.","u":"https://www.ndcourts.gov/legal-resources/rules/ndrcivp"},{"n":"71","t":"Process for and against nonparties","s":"Orders benefiting or binding nonparties are enforced as against parties.","u":"https://www.ndcourts.gov/legal-resources/rules/ndrcivp"},{"n":"77","t":"District courts and clerks","s":"District courts are always open for filing and process. Trials on the merits are in open court. The clerk may issue process and act on matters not requiring a judge.","u":"https://www.ndcourts.gov/legal-resources/rules/ndrcivp"},{"n":"80","t":"Transcript as evidence","s":"Admissible prior testimony may be proved by a certified transcript.","u":"https://www.ndcourts.gov/legal-resources/rules/ndrcivp"},{"n":"81","t":"Applicability of the rules in general","s":"Special statutory proceedings (Table A lists many) are excluded to the extent inconsistent with the rules. The rules do not supersede statutes on appeals to the district court except where not in conflict. When a statute says to act as in a civil action, the rules apply.","u":"https://www.ndcourts.gov/legal-resources/rules/ndrcivp"},{"n":"82","t":"[Reserved]","s":"No text.","u":"https://www.ndcourts.gov/legal-resources/rules/ndrcivp"},{"n":"83","t":"Practice when procedure is not specified","s":"Judicial districts may adopt local rules not inconsistent with statewide rules, referred to the Supreme Court for review. Otherwise the district court may regulate its practice consistently with rules and statutes.","u":"https://www.ndcourts.gov/legal-resources/rules/ndrcivp"},{"n":"84","t":"Appendix of forms","s":"The appendix forms suffice under the rules.","u":"https://www.ndcourts.gov/legal-resources/rules/ndrcivp"},{"n":"85","t":"Title and citation","s":"Cited as “N.D.R.Civ.P.”","u":"https://www.ndcourts.gov/legal-resources/rules/ndrcivp"},{"n":"86","t":"Effective date; statutes superseded","s":"Amendments take effect as ordered and apply to pending cases unless infeasible or unjust. Conflicting statutes are superseded and listed in the rule’s explanatory note and in Table B.","u":"https://www.ndcourts.gov/legal-resources/rules/ndrcivp"}]});
